Errmmm, well, here it is anyway...

I finally got around to boxing a new release of SVGATextMode. Phew...

The files are as follows:

  266907 SVGATextMode-1.5-dos.tar.gz
  221503 SVGATextMode-1.5-bin.tar.gz
  362224 SVGATextMode-1.5-src.tar.gz
    2123 SVGATextMode.lsm


Here's the new features compared to the 1.4 release (Sept 22, 1996):

15 APR 1997 - version 1.5

- - Better textmode restoration for Tseng chips (when recovering from a
  graphics-program crash).

- - fix endless-loop bug when resizing from a list of VTs (= when "Terminals"
  line used in config file).

- - Trident TGUI support (Massimiliano Ghilardi).

- - Integration of XFree 3.2 clock code. This may cause some new-and-improved
  bugs. Now where's that bug spray I bought last week...

- - 9-pixel wide characters now correctly display "box" characters (Stuart
  Lamble)

- - Matrox Millennium support (Trey Boudreau). I've been getting more and
  more requests for Millennium support, and thanks to Trey they'll all
  finally stop whining :-)  Supreme work!

- - MACH64CT support (Matthias Grimrath). Other MACH64 chips are not supported
  yet. Who will follow the footsteps of the now-famous people that have
  contributed support for a new chip? Have you considered the ego-boosting
  possibilities of this?

- - Accept range of frequencies when only one single frequency is given in
  config file. This reflects the reality of so-called "fixed-frequency"
  monitors (i.e. they can still cope with a small range of frequencies,
  despite their name).

- - replaced misleading "CLGD542x" chipset with more general "CIRRUS" chipset.
  Be warned, Cirrus users: you'll need to change your Chipset string!
  Too many people concluded that their "CL-GD543x" wasn't suppoted...


Here's a "wazzup?" file for those with short memories:

  Begin3
  Title: SVGA textmode manipulation/enhancement
  Version: 1.5
  Entered-date: 15APR96
  Description: Uses extra features found on SVGA video cards (high pixel
               clocks) to enhance Linux text modes, independently of what the
               BIOS can do. It is configured with an XF86Config-like file, and
               allows setting of pixel clock, H/V timings, font size, cursor
               size, etc.
               Enables using the video card AND the monitor to their full
               potential in textmode, as with the XFree86 X-server. Current
               support includes S3, ET3000, ET4000, ET6000, Cirrus Logic,
               Trident, Western Digital, ATI, ATI MACH8, MACH32 and MACH64CT,
               Video7 (Headland), Avance Logic (ALI), Oak Technology (OTI),
               SiS, RealTek, ARK, NCR, Genoa (GVGA), MX and Matrox Millennium
               cards.
               It can also use an external clock program and supports all S3
               clock chips from XFREE 3.2. Features automatic font loading,
               monitor definition (H/V frequency limits), on-the-fly screen
               resizing, cursor shape definition, ...
               Also includes: palette changer, pixelclock probe, text/graphics
               mode grabber, VGA register hacking tool, and a multi-chipset/
               clockchip ClockProg for X that supports ALL chipsets and
               clockchips from SVGATextMode.
               Binaries for Linux-ELF and DOS included for SVGATextMode and
               grabmode.
  Keywords: SVGA VGA console screen monitor XFree86 Xconfig XF86Config
  Author: koen.gadeyne@barco.com (Koen Gadeyne)
  Maintained-by: koen.gadeyne@barco.com (Koen Gadeyne)
  Primary-site: tsx-11.mit.edu /pub/linux/sources/sbin
                362k SVGATextMode-1.5-src.tar.gz
                221k SVGATextMode-1.5-bin.tar.gz
                266k SVGATextMode-1.5-dos.tar.gz
  Alternate-site: sunsite.unc.edu /pub/Linux/utils/console
  Original-site:
  Platforms: Linux machine (Intel or Alpha) with one of the supported VGA
             cards, kernel 1.1.54 or newer.
  Copying-policy: GNU General Public License (GPL) version 2
  End


And what about the Mystery card? Hold your breath... It's coming... soon.


Now let's hope this clunker doesn't have too many bugs.

Koen.


