I am happy to announce a new release of the e2fsprogs distribution.
All users of e2fsprogs are urged to upgrade to the 1.09 version as
soon as possible.

This release is primarily a performance and bug-fix release over 1.07,
the last publically announced release.  The most major performance
improvement was in e2fsck's handling of news spool filesystems (very
large filesystems with many hard-linked files).  The reduction in
e2fsck's memory footprint in 1.07 triggered a performance problem which
was addressed in this release.

The e2fsck program now also correctly handles some types of corruption
where directories were cloned during pass 1b, and those directories
contained corrupted directory entries.  Previously the wrong version
of the directory blocks would get fixed, thus forcing another e2fsck
pass before the filesystem was fully fixed.  In addition, e2fsck now
tries to print the correct superblock number for "non-standard"
filesystems (i.e., filesystems using 2k or 4k blocks, or an unusual
number of blocks per group).

The mke2fs program now avoids locating the block and inode bitmaps in
the same relative positions on every block group; this avoids "hot
spots" in certain RAID configurations.

The dumpe2fs program now prints more information about the filesystem,
including the filesystem revision, any features which might be enabled,
and the offsets from the beginning of the block group for the inode
table and bitmap structures.

This release also has a number of more minor enhancements and bug
fixes; for more details, see the release notes.

E2fsprogs is available in source form, as well precompiled i386
Linux/ELF binaries, and as RPM source and binary packages.  You can
obtain from the following locations:

	http://web.mit.edu/tytso/www/linux/e2fsprogs.html

or

	ftp://tsx-11.mit.edu/pub/linux/packages/ext2fs

The RPM files (including the source SRPM file) are digitally signed
using PGP.  You may also verify the MD5 checksums below:

