To allow for easier expandability, I have changed the parsing engine from a
homebrew strange one to one using code generated by Flex and Bison (Lex and
Yacc may also work -- I don't know if I used any Flex or Bison specific
functionality).

This unfortunately may cause a slightly more CPU hungry program when diald is
keeping track of many packets.  I'll be working on this.  To allow for the
user to choose how responsive the program is, I have implemented a runtime
configuration option to change the length of the sleep taken between reads of
the monitor pipe.

The changes since v0.7 are:


 o Changed sleeps to be runtime changable.  Defaults to 100ms.
 o Changed parsing engine to use Flex & Bison (as in Lex & Yacc) to allow for
	easier expansion and maintainablility.

Since some people may not have Flex or Bison installed, I have included the 
generated code in the archive, along with a prebuilt ELF binary dynamically
linked with ncurses-1.9.9e.   Enjoy.

Please send any comments, bug reports or patches to:
	gjhurlbu@beirdo.uplink.on.ca




