-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----


First public release of word2x

word2x is a word 6 to anything convert supplied with text and latex
output formats. It guesses rather a lot from context due to the
opaqueness of the word format itself. Pictures and other junk are
skipped but occasionally a few printable items creep in somehow.

The LaTeX output has very limited equation support since I had few
example to go on (only fractions and guesswork). It did manage to use
eqnarry* for something the word viewer render as several lines of
display style. Since I do not know anything about front change pounds
signs are sometimes rendered as =< or $\leq$. The LaTeX also spots
bullets and switches into itemize! *'s in maths mode are render as
\times which IMHO looks much better than what word viewer uses.

Tables are supported and with luck it will guess the alignment
correctly.  Columns witch are mostly numbers get left (non-numbers)
and decimal point (numbers) alignment, which apparently word can not
cope with---no document I have seen does this using word
viewer. (Decimal point alignment is a matter of a little \phantom for
the missing sign and leading 0's).

You probably have to do minor cleaning up after this program but it
beats strings (and even catdoc) by a long way. There are lots of
things missing, which patches are information to rectify are
welcomed. These include:

* Much equation support

* Automatic paragraph fields for long entries in LaTeX output tables
  (already done this for text mode).

* Page breaks

* Headers and footers

* Font changes

* Equation support in text mode

* Better understanding of the word format to improve conversion
  accuracy.

* Pleny of other items.


Copyright.

word2x is (C) D.P.Simpson 1997. Copying policy is GPL version 2 (or
any later version at your option).


Obtaining word2x

word2x is coming to a CTAN archive near you in the tools directory.
It has appeared at ftp.tex.ac.uk already (its initial point of
release).  It should appear in the word format conversion FAQ sometime
(not something I had heard of but worth knowing about...)

You also need getopt_long, already included in Linux C libraries, and
a C++ compiler (g++ worked nicely for me). Users of my previous
pre-pre-release will find this version offers extra features and
various improvements (from minor to major, depending on which
pre-pre-prelease they got) over the version they have already.

Authors email address:

The author is reachable as dps@duncan.telstar.net (this is an
automatic forwarding address). PGP public key aviable by emailing
pgp@duncan.telstar.net (automatic reply, subject and body irrelevant).
[As with finger only the admins (me and a friend) and the person who
owns the virtual site (me) can change the message---a user password or
admin access is required. telstar.net is a comercial service, powered
by Linux (on a 486DX4/100 w. 16Mb)]

